description:
Turn a list of memory accesses seen through one pointer into a C++ struct definition, as lesson 2.04 does by hand:

```cpp
struct Access { std::uint32_t offset; std::uint32_t size; bool isFloat; bool isSigned; };

std::string recoverStruct(const std::string& name, std::vector<Access> accesses, std::uint32_t totalSize);
```

- Sort accesses by offset. Accesses at the **same** offset merge into one field: the largest size wins, and it is floating point if any of them was, signed if any was.
- An access that starts inside the previous field is ignored.
- Field types: size 1 `std::int8_t`/`std::uint8_t`, 2 `std::int16_t`/`std::uint16_t`, 4 `std::int32_t`/`std::uint32_t` or `float`, 8 `std::int64_t`/`std::uint64_t` or `double`. Signed types when `isSigned`; `float`/`double` when `isFloat`.
- Fields are named `field_` and the offset as at least two uppercase hex digits: `field_0C`, `field_1A0`.
- A gap before a field, or between the last field and `totalSize`, becomes `std::uint8_t pad_XX[n];` with `XX` the gap's offset written the same way.

The output is exactly:

```text
struct Player
{
    std::uint8_t pad_00[12];
    std::int32_t field_0C;
    float field_10;
};
```

with four spaces before each member and a newline after every line, including the last.

